lyze this situation carefully.\n\n**Current Situation:**\n- My hand: K♦ 10♦\n- Board (Flop): Q♠ 2♥ 8♦\n- Pot history: I raised 200 pre-flop, opponent called. I checked the flop, opponent bet 300.\n- I need to call 300 to continue.\n\n**Hand Analysis:**\n- Currently: King-high (very weak)\n- Outs for improvement:\n  - 4 Jacks would give me a straight (K-Q-J-10-9 if a 9 also comes, or Q-J-10-9-8)\n  - Wait, let me recalculate: For K-Q-J-10-9, I'd need J and 9 (8 outs for two specific cards)\n  - Actually, I need J-9 specifically for a straight, which is unlikely\n  - 3 Kings would give me top pair (but opponent could have better)\n  - 3 Tens would give me middle pair (weak)\n\n**Opponent's Action:**\n- Called my pre-flop raise (showing some strength)\n- Bet 300 on the flop (could be a continuation bet, could have hit something)\n- Possible hands: Pair of Queens (strong), a pair like 88 or 22 (sets), any pocket pair, Ace-high, draws\n\n**Pot Odds:**\n- Need to call 300 to win approximately 1,100 (200+200+300+300 from previous actions + blinds)\n- Getting about 3.7:1 odds\n- With just overcards and a gutshot possibility, my equity is probably around 20-30% at best\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n- I'm ahead overall (+2,450 cumulative)\n- This is hand 15/20, so position matters but not critical yet\n- K-high on this board is very weak\n- Opponent showed strength by betting into me after I checked\n- I'd be calling 300 with a marginal hand and poor position\n\n**Options:**\n1. **Fold (-1)**: Lose 200 chips invested, preserve 19,750\n2. **Call (0)**: Risk 300 more, unlikely to improve significantly\n3. **Raise (600+)**: Would be a bluff with a weak hand, risky\n\nReason: \nMy King-high has minimal value against an opponent who bet the flop. I have no made hand, no strong draw (only backdoor straight possibilities), and poor pot odds for my limited outs. Calling 300 with such a weak holding would be throwing good money after bad. Since I'm already ahead in cumulative chip